The Security Council,

Recalling all its previous resolutions and statements of its President on the situation in Somalia, in particular resolutions 2710 (2023), 2714 (2023), 2741 (2024), 2747 (2024), 2748 (2024), 2753 (2024) and 2767 (2024),

Reaffirming its full respect for the sovereignty, territorial integrity, political independence and unity of Somalia, and its commitment to the peace, security and development of Somalia in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations,

Recalling that the Federal Government of Somalia has primary responsibility for ensuring security within Somalia, and recognizing the Federal Government’s request for continued international support and the importance of the Somali Security Development Plan and the National Security Architecture,

Commending the contribution to peace and security in Somalia made by the African Union Mission in Somalia, the African Union Transition Mission in Somalia and the African Union Support and Stabilization Mission in Somalia (AUSSOM), and paying tribute to all those who have served and sacrificed,

Welcoming the deployment of AUSSOM since 1 January 2025 and the efforts of the African Union, troop- and police-contributing countries, the Federal Government of Somalia and the federal member states to counter Al-Shabaab and Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant (ISIL/Da’esh) affiliates,

Noting that the mandate of AUSSOM, as set out in resolution 2767 (2024), expires on 31 December 2025, and underscoring the need for timely renewal and for a clear, conditions-based approach to the progressive transfer of security responsibilities to Somali forces,

Expressing grave concern at the continuing terrorist threat posed by Al-Shabaab and ISIL/Da’esh affiliates, condemning in the strongest terms terrorist attacks in Somalia and neighbouring States, and expressing deep concern at the loss of civilian life,

Expressing serious concern about the humanitarian situation in Somalia, including drought, flooding, displacement and food insecurity, and calling for safe, timely, sustained and unhindered humanitarian access to all persons in need,

Emphasizing the importance of a holistic, whole-of-government and whole-of-society approach to counter terrorism and violent extremism conducive to terrorism, including by addressing governance, security, human rights, humanitarian, development and socio-economic dimensions, and the importance of reconciliation, justice and the rule of law,

Reaffirming the importance of the full, equal, meaningful and safe participation of women in all efforts for the maintenance and promotion of peace and security, and the need to protect children and prevent conflict-related sexual violence and other violations and abuses of human rights and violations of international humanitarian law,

Recognizing the complementary and mutually reinforcing mandates of AUSSOM and the United Nations Transitional Assistance Mission in Somalia, and the importance of close coordination among the United Nations, the African Union, the Federal Government of Somalia, the federal member states and international partners,

Recalling resolution 2719 (2023) and stressing the need for predictable, sustainable and flexible financing for African Union-led peace support operations authorized by the Security Council, including AUSSOM,

Welcoming regional diplomatic efforts to reduce tensions and encouraging all States in the region to resolve disputes peacefully and in full respect for the sovereignty, territorial integrity and political independence of Somalia,

Determining that the situation in Somalia continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Authorizes the Member States of the African Union to maintain the deployment of the African Union Support and Stabilization Mission in Somalia (AUSSOM) until 31 December 2026, with up to 12,626 uniformed personnel, including a minimum of 1,040 police personnel, consistent with resolution 2767 (2024) and the African Union Concept of Operations;

2. Authorizes AUSSOM, within the limit of its capabilities and areas of deployment, to take all necessary measures to support the Federal Government of Somalia and the federal member states in:
(a) implementing the Somali Security Development Plan and the National Security Architecture, including through joint operations against Al-Shabaab and ISIL/Da’esh affiliates, degrading their operational capacity, countering improvised explosive devices, disrupting terrorist financing and arms trafficking, and securing key population centres, supply routes and infrastructure;
(b) protecting civilians under imminent threat of physical violence, without prejudice to the primary responsibility of the Federal Government of Somalia;
(c) facilitating safe, unhindered and timely humanitarian access and the delivery of humanitarian assistance;
(d) supporting stabilization, reconciliation, local governance, the rule of law and civilian protection, including, where conditions permit, the voluntary return of displaced persons;
(e) building the capacity of Somali security forces and institutions, including through training, mentoring, joint planning and advisory support, with a view to the progressive transfer of security responsibilities;
(f) supporting, upon request, the creation of a secure environment for inclusive elections and constitutional processes;
(g) protecting United Nations and African Union personnel, facilities, installations and equipment;

3. Requests AUSSOM, in close coordination with the Federal Government of Somalia and the federal member states, to implement its mandate with full respect for international humanitarian law, international human rights law and refugee law, and to apply its compliance framework, including on conduct and discipline, protection of children, the prevention of conflict-related sexual violence and the deployment of women and gender advisers;

4. Calls upon the Federal Government of Somalia and the federal member states to cooperate fully with AUSSOM, resolve political disputes peacefully, including through dialogue and the federal constitutional process, and take concrete steps on national priorities, including security sector reform, federalism, fiscal federalism, justice and police, reconciliation and free and fair elections;

5. Condemns all terrorist attacks, acts of violence and violations of international humanitarian law and human rights abuses in Somalia, demands that all parties cease attacks against civilians, humanitarian personnel and peacekeepers, and calls upon all Member States to fully implement the Somalia arms embargo and to prevent the diversion of weapons and ammunition;

6. Emphasizes the need for the United Nations, the African Union and the Federal Government of Somalia to enhance coordination and information-sharing, and requests the Secretary-General to continue to provide, through the United Nations Support Office in Somalia and within existing mandates and resources, logistical, administrative and security support to AUSSOM;

7. Reaffirms the importance of the modalities set out in resolution 2719 (2023); stresses the need for predictable, sustainable and flexible financing for AUSSOM; requests the Secretary-General, in coordination with the African Union Commission and the Federal Government of Somalia, to submit to the Council by 31 March 2026 a joint costed proposal, including options for applying resolution 2719 (2023) to AUSSOM; and affirms its intention to consider that proposal as a matter of priority;

8. Calls upon the international community to scale up support for the humanitarian response in Somalia, to address the impacts of climate change, drought and flooding, and to support resilience, recovery and development in line with Somali priorities;

9. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council on the implementation of this resolution, including on the political, security, humanitarian and human rights situation and on compliance with international humanitarian law and human rights law, by 31 March 2026 and every 120 days thereafter;

10. Requests the African Union to keep the Council informed, through the Secretary-General, of the implementation of AUSSOM’s mandate and to coordinate its reporting with that of the Secretary-General;

11. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

