(d) supporting stabilization, reconciliation, local governance, the rule of law and civilian protection, including, where conditions permit, the voluntary return of displaced persons;
(e) building the capacity of Somali security forces and institutions, including through training, mentoring, joint planning and advisory support, with a view to the progressive transfer of security responsibilities;
(f) supporting, upon request of the Federal Government of Somalia, the creation of a secure environment for inclusive national elections and constitutional processes in accordance with Somali law and the Constitution;
(g) protecting United Nations and African Union personnel, facilities, installations and equipment;

5. Requests AUSSOM, in close coordination with the Federal Government of Somalia and the federal member states, to implement its mandate with full respect for international humanitarian law, international human rights law and refugee law, and to apply its compliance framework, including the United Nations Human Rights Due Diligence Policy, provisions on conduct and discipline, protection of children, the prevention of conflict-related sexual violence, the deployment of women and gender advisers and child protection advisers, and a civilian harm mitigation and response framework, including regular reporting on civilian casualties and prompt investigation of allegations of misconduct and violations;

6. Requests AUSSOM to ensure the full, equal, meaningful and safe participation of women in all aspects of its mandate, including through gender-responsive conflict analysis and reporting to the Council on progress in this regard;

7. Calls upon the Federal Government of Somalia and the federal member states to cooperate fully with AUSSOM, resolve political disputes peacefully, including through dialogue and the federal constitutional process, and take concrete steps on national priorities, including security sector reform, federalism, fiscal federalism, justice and police, reconciliation and free and fair elections with the full, equal, meaningful and safe participation of women and youth;

8. Calls upon all States in the region to respect the sovereignty, territorial integrity, political independence and unity of Somalia and to resolve disputes peacefully through dialogue and in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations, including by refraining from unilateral actions that could undermine Somalia’s sovereignty and territorial integrity;

9. Condemns all terrorist attacks, acts of violence and violations of international humanitarian law and human rights abuses in Somalia, demands that all parties cease attacks against civilians, humanitarian personnel and peacekeepers, calls upon all Member States to fully implement the relevant Security Council resolutions, in particular resolution 2714 (2023), and to prevent the diversion of weapons and ammunition to Al-Shabaab and other armed groups; reaffirms the right of the Federal Government of Somalia to acquire and import arms and ammunition for its security forces, consistent with applicable Council resolutions; and stresses the need to ensure accountability for violations and abuses of human rights and violations of international humanitarian law;

10. Emphasizes the need for the United Nations, the African Union and the Federal Government of Somalia to enhance coordination and information-sharing, and requests the Secretary-General to continue to provide, through the United Nations Support Office in Somalia and within existing mandates and resources, logistical, administrative and security support to AUSSOM, in close consultation with troop- and police-contributing countries, including on mandate implementation, force protection and drawdown planning, and requests that, where possible and in line with United Nations rules and procedures, procurement for AUSSOM be maximized from the local Somali market in order to support the local economy;

11. Reaffirms the importance of the modalities set out in resolution 2719 (2023), calls for their full and timely implementation for AUSSOM, and stresses the need for predictable, sustainable, flexible and adequate financing for AUSSOM, including through the accelerated implementation of the Joint African Union–United Nations Roadmap on the Operationalization of resolution 2719 (2023), voluntary contributions, burden-sharing among all partners, and, as appropriate, United Nations assessed contributions subject to a separate decision of the Security Council; calls upon the European Union and other traditional and new partners to remain engaged; requests the Secretary-General, in coordination with the African Union Commission and the Federal Government of Somalia, to submit to the Council by 31 January 2026 a joint costed proposal, including concrete modalities for applying resolution 2719 (2023) to AUSSOM, with a view to ensuring predictable, sustainable, flexible and adequate financing, including through the potential use of United Nations assessed contributions subject to a separate decision of the Security Council, and an assessment of the African Union’s ability to fund at least 25 per cent of the mission’s costs, in full respect of the budgetary procedures and prerogatives of the United Nations General Assembly; decides to take a decision on the financing modalities for AUSSOM, in line with resolution 2719 (2023), within 60 days of the submission of the joint costed proposal and in any event by 31 May 2026;

12. Calls upon the international community to scale up support for the humanitarian response in Somalia, to address the impacts of drought, flooding and other natural disasters, including through support for early warning systems, and to support resilience, recovery and development in line with Somali priorities;

13. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council on the implementation of this resolution, including on the political, security, humanitarian and human rights situation, climate-related security risks, progress against conditions-based benchmarks for the progressive transfer of security responsibilities to Somali forces, the implementation of the compliance framework and the United Nations Human Rights Due Diligence Policy, incidents of civilian harm, and compliance with international humanitarian law and human rights law, by 31 March 2026 and every 90 days thereafter;

14. Requests the African Union to keep the Council informed, through the Secretary-General, of the implementation of AUSSOM’s mandate and to coordinate its reporting with that of the Secretary-General;

15. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.
